<div class="ed-reading guide-reading text-lg">
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">A frightening message does not always mean your computer has a virus. A web page can imitate a security alert, a desktop notification can come from a website, and a genuine protection tool can report a detected file. Slowness can also have many causes. Start by recording what you actually observed rather than naming the problem too soon. This guide is for one working personal Windows or Mac computer. It is not a ransomware recovery plan, a failed-drive procedure or a substitute for urgent help.</p>
{% call note("care", "Take care", "Urgent incidents come first") %}<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If files are being encrypted or a ransom note appears, stop these routine checks. Stop using the device, disconnect it from the network and seek the <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://www.cyber.gov.au/report-and-recover/recover-from/ransomware">ACSC ransomware response guidance</a> from another trusted device. Disconnection alone is not complete containment. If money is exposed, contact your bank immediately using independently verified official details.</p>{% endcall %}
